  "body": " Writing Functions with for Loops   Approximating Pi   Rewrite the for -loop above as a while -loop that computes the first 10 terms of the Leibniz series. Verify that both versions give the same result.      Approximating Pi   In the previous section , you approximated using the Leibniz formula: with a for -loop:  N = 10; total = 0; for k = 1:N total = total + (-1)^(k+1) * 1\/(2*k - 1); end approx = total * 4; fprintf(' pi ≈ %10.8g\\n', pi); fprintf('approx of pi ≈ %10.8g\\n', approx);  Work through the tasks below to convert this to a while -loop.  Now update your while -loop so that instead of stopping after a fixed number of terms, it keeps adding terms until the approximation is within 0.001 of pi . Track the number of terms needed in N .      Approximating Pi   Introduce a variable called tol to represent the closeness tolerance. Experiment with the different tolerances: 0.0001 and 0.00001 . How does the number of terms change?   Notice that each extra digit of accuracy requires roughly 10 times as many terms. This series converges very slowly.    N = 10; total = 0; %─── TYPE YOUR CODE HERE ───% tol = 0.001; distFromPi = Inf; while distFromPi > tol N = N + 1; total = total + (-1)^(N+1) * 1\/(2*N - 1); distFromPi = abs(4 * total - pi); end %───────────────────────────% approx = total * 4; fprintf(' pi ≈ %10.8g\\n', pi); fprintf('approx of pi ≈ %10.8g\\n', approx); fprintf('with tolerance = %10.8g\\n', tol); fprintf('terms needed = %i\\n', N);      Write a function doubleUntil(x, N) that uses a while -loop to determine how many times x must be doubled before it exceeds N . Return the number of doublings and the final value.  For example, if and , then repeatedly doubling gives: . So, was doubled times and the final value was .        Inputs:  x  (1x1) double  positive starting value     N  (1x1) double  threshold to exceed    Outputs:  count  (1x1) double  number of doublings performed     final  (1x1) double  final value after the last doubling      Test Cases ⤵︎  [c, v] = doubleUntil(5, 76) [c, v] = doubleUntil(13.2, 52.7) [c, v] = doubleUntil(3, 2)    Expected ⤵︎  c = 4 v = 80 c = 2 v = 52.8 c = 0 v = 3        Write a function numDigits(n) that takes a positive integer n and returns the number of digits in n .  Use a while -loop that repeatedly divides the number by 10 (moving the decimal point one place left) and counts how many times this can be done before the number becomes less than 1.        Inputs:  n  (1x1) double  positive integer    Outputs:  count  (1x1) double  number of digits in n      Test Cases ⤵︎  count = numDigits(7) count = numDigits(42) count = numDigits(10000) count = numDigits(938271)    Expected ⤵︎  count = 1 count = 2 count = 5 count = 6        Write a function yearsToDouble(rate) that determines how many years it takes for an investment to double at a given annual interest rate. Each year, the balance grows by balance = balance * (1 + rate) . Assume an initial balance of $1.        Inputs:  rate  (1x1) double  annual interest rate as a decimal (e.g., 0.05 for 5%)    Outputs:  years  (1x1) double  number of years to at least double the investment      Test Cases ⤵︎  years = yearsToDouble(0.05) years = yearsToDouble(0.10) years = yearsToDouble(0.01)    Expected ⤵︎  years = 15 years = 8 years = 70        Write a function called makeChange that breaks a number of cents into quarters, dimes, nickels, and pennies using the greedy algorithm.  Use a separate while -loop for each coin type. Start with quarters, then dimes, then nickels, and finally pennies. Return the number of each coin used and totalCoins , the total number of coins in the final breakdown.  For U.S. coins, this greedy process gives the fewest coins possible.        Inputs:  amount  (1x1) double  nonnegative integer    Outputs:  q  (1x1) double  number of quarters used     d  (1x1) double  number of dimes used     n  (1x1) double  number of nickels used     p  (1x1) double  number of pennies used     change  (1x1) double  total number of coins used      Test Cases ⤵︎  [q, d, n, p, tot] = makeChange(99) [q, d, n, p, tot] = makeChange(41) [q, d, n, p, tot] = makeChange(30)    Expected ⤵︎  q=3, d=2, n=0, p=4, tot=9 q=1, d=1, n=1, p=1, tot=4 q=1, d=0, n=1, p=0, tot=2      "
